<article class="env-definition" id="09CI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09CI">Definition <span data-tag="09CI">10.133.1</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $M$, $N$ be $S$-modules. Let $k \geq 0$ be an integer. We inductively define a <em>differential operator $D : M \to N$ of order $k$</em> to be an $R$-linear map such that for all $g \in S$ the map $m \mapsto D(gm) - gD(m)$ is a differential operator of order $k - 1$. For the base case $k = 0$ we define a differential operator of order $0$ to be an $S$-linear map. </p>
</article>